WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.261 --> 00:00:11.591
People say that life is short and
that we should enjoy it to the fullest.

00:00:11.591 --> 00:00:14.633
I don't really deny that fact
that we should enjoy our life,

00:00:14.633 --> 00:00:17.552
but short is not the word 
that I would use.

00:00:17.552 --> 00:00:22.282
I think of life like a dish
with so many flavors to go around,

00:00:22.282 --> 00:00:25.242
like sweet for those amazing moments,

00:00:25.242 --> 00:00:29.513
and bitter for those 
not so amazing moments.

00:00:29.513 --> 00:00:33.923
Today, I am here to share with you
a few of the lessons that I learned

00:00:33.923 --> 00:00:36.314
because of my passion for cooking.

00:00:36.314 --> 00:00:38.240
Often when children are rather young,

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:43.142
they don't really know what they
want to do in life, but I always have.

00:00:43.142 --> 00:00:46.064
I have always wanted to become a chef.

00:00:46.064 --> 00:00:48.383
I felt happy when I worked with flavor

00:00:48.383 --> 00:00:51.383
and that's when I knew 
I had to become a chef.

00:00:51.383 --> 00:00:55.133
I looked at life through the eyes of
food and flavor,

00:00:55.133 --> 00:00:58.873
and I learned some very interesting things
in very interesting ways

00:00:58.873 --> 00:01:00.943
that I am here to share with you.

00:01:00.943 --> 00:01:02.493
When I was eight years old,

00:01:02.493 --> 00:01:06.494
I noticed that even though
a chocolate cake and a chocolate pudding

00:01:06.494 --> 00:01:08.944
are made of the same exact 
core ingredient,

00:01:08.944 --> 00:01:11.564
they taste so different 
from each other, right?

00:01:11.564 --> 00:01:16.374
Each ingredient, a single ingredient,
is so different

00:01:16.374 --> 00:01:19.453
that food is all about choices

00:01:19.453 --> 00:01:22.996
and there is so much that you can do
with a single ingredient

00:01:22.996 --> 00:01:25.944
because every time that you pair
it with something different,

00:01:25.944 --> 00:01:29.226
it turns out as something totally new.

00:01:29.226 --> 00:01:33.035
For example, when you take chocolate
and you pair it with mint,

00:01:33.035 --> 00:01:35.445
it's rather refreshing, right?

00:01:35.445 --> 00:01:39.336
But if you pair it with spice, instead,
it's bolder.

00:01:39.336 --> 00:01:44.527
I also realized that when certain 
ingredients are in certain dishes,

00:01:44.527 --> 00:01:46.526
they taste great.

00:01:46.526 --> 00:01:49.916
But when this same ingredient
is in another dish,

00:01:49.916 --> 00:01:53.906
it tastes so different, maybe even bad.

00:01:53.906 --> 00:01:57.506
When I was nine years old,
I had about enough of waiting, okay?

00:01:57.506 --> 00:02:00.946
And I thought it's about time
I start cooking on my own.

00:02:00.946 --> 00:02:04.746
So, I decided that for the first time,
I was going to make a dessert.

00:02:04.746 --> 00:02:08.916
And I was so excited to finally
start making something with my own hands,

00:02:08.916 --> 00:02:11.916
I could have a creation to show people
that they could taste.

00:02:11.916 --> 00:02:15.736
In my head, it all looked like
one of those pictures from a recipe book

00:02:15.736 --> 00:02:18.877
that's all garnished and perfect.

00:02:18.877 --> 00:02:21.788
But I didn't really do that much
of research to find out

00:02:21.788 --> 00:02:24.037
the kinds of ingredients
that I would need

00:02:24.037 --> 00:02:26.476
to obtain such an amazing dish.

00:02:26.476 --> 00:02:30.418
I thought I had a fair idea,
which I totally did not.

00:02:30.418 --> 00:02:33.828
So I just randomly started grabbing
ingredients from around my kitchen

00:02:33.828 --> 00:02:37.768
like cocoa powder, sugar,
baking soda and some chocolate.

00:02:37.768 --> 00:02:40.918
I mixed it all together in a bowl
and called it a mousse.

00:02:40.918 --> 00:02:44.368
But here's a tip: you do not add
baking soda to something

00:02:44.368 --> 00:02:46.868
unless you're going to bake it.

00:02:46.868 --> 00:02:50.019
Otherwise, it's going to taste like sand.

00:02:50.019 --> 00:02:51.500
No kidding.

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:55.949
So my dish was trash, 
but this was not a failure,

00:02:55.949 --> 00:02:57.458
it was a lesson,

00:02:57.458 --> 00:03:01.209
a lesson that I could learn from
and a mistake that I could fix.

00:03:01.209 --> 00:03:04.659
And what I learned from this is that
if you fail,

00:03:04.659 --> 00:03:07.990
it's pretty much taught you something
along the way

00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:12.300
and you just need to search for it
and you need to choose to learn from it.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:17.093
Have you ever mixed any two flavors
that are not supposed to be mixed together

00:03:17.093 --> 00:03:20.089
but actually turn out 
to taste really great?

00:03:20.089 --> 00:03:22.546
Well, I have, and it did turn out great.

00:03:22.546 --> 00:03:26.697
Chocolate with a pinch of salt in it -
it doesn't actually taste that bad.

00:03:26.697 --> 00:03:29.280
I know it sounds weird,
but it doesn't taste that bad.

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:32.019
People just assume that some things
don't work together,

00:03:32.019 --> 00:03:34.880
not realizing that each 
of these ingredients

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:38.090
has their own unique, 
very adventurous flavors

00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:40.610
that they could unleash
which they just do not

00:03:40.610 --> 00:03:45.560
and do not realize that they could
create something totally new.

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:48.111
For this, I take you into 
the Indian kitchen

00:03:48.111 --> 00:03:52.401
where every household in India
has one circular metal box

00:03:52.401 --> 00:03:56.523
with five smaller circular metal bowls
inside of it.

00:03:56.523 --> 00:03:59.401
Each of these bowls has a different
spice in it.

00:03:59.401 --> 00:04:01.613
This is called the masala dani.

00:04:01.613 --> 00:04:06.701
Households in different parts of India
use different kinds of spices,

00:04:06.701 --> 00:04:11.870
which causes for this masala dani
to differ in case of various families.

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:14.231
But still, each and every time,

00:04:14.231 --> 00:04:17.022
each of these unique and diverse
masala danis

00:04:17.022 --> 00:04:19.571
comes out with an amazing dish.

00:04:19.571 --> 00:04:21.333
This fascinated me a lot,

00:04:21.333 --> 00:04:26.932
and I noticed that each of these spices
has its own characteristics,

00:04:26.932 --> 00:04:29.083
it's diverse in its own way,

00:04:29.083 --> 00:04:33.511
and they still compliment
each other while adding to the dish

00:04:33.511 --> 00:04:35.403
all at the same time.

00:04:35.403 --> 00:04:39.164
Each of these unique spices
and each of these unique masala danis

00:04:39.164 --> 00:04:45.173
just harmonize all together to give
this box a beautiful and unique aroma.

00:04:45.173 --> 00:04:49.023
And maybe, if we all worked that way, too,
and harmonized,

00:04:49.023 --> 00:04:54.103
we could give our world a beautiful
and unique aroma of positivity.

00:04:54.103 --> 00:04:58.383
When you taste a dish, 
you taste many flavors

00:04:58.383 --> 00:05:01.803
but there are certain flavors
that stand out more,

00:05:01.803 --> 00:05:04.538
that are bolder, like spice.

00:05:04.538 --> 00:05:06.904
When I was eleven years old

00:05:06.904 --> 00:05:10.114
and in the process of making
my original TED-Ed talk,

00:05:10.114 --> 00:05:15.364
I was newly learning to present
a talk more efficiently,

00:05:15.364 --> 00:05:19.735
and that's when I realized that
a talk is pretty much a bunch of words

00:05:19.735 --> 00:05:22.093
without the passion in them,

00:05:22.093 --> 00:05:26.564
the same way that a recipe is nothing
without the flavor in them.

00:05:26.564 --> 00:05:29.075
How I see it, my life is a cake,

00:05:29.075 --> 00:05:33.214
and I can choose to add
whatever flavor I want to it.

00:05:33.214 --> 00:05:37.885
If people happen to not like my flavor,
well, you know what? That's okay.

00:05:37.885 --> 00:05:41.865
But that does not mean
that I need to stop expressing my flavor

00:05:41.865 --> 00:05:46.135
because no matter what flavor,
cake always tastes great.

00:05:46.135 --> 00:05:49.176
I have a dream like everybody
in this room does.

00:05:49.176 --> 00:05:50.647
Everybody has a dream.

00:05:50.647 --> 00:05:53.316
I decided to look at life
through the eyes of it,

00:05:53.316 --> 00:05:55.284
and I learned to stand out,

00:05:55.284 --> 00:05:56.807
learn from my failures,

00:05:56.807 --> 00:05:59.616
and make my own choices.

00:05:59.616 --> 00:06:01.987
Throughout this journey 
of realizing my dream,

00:06:01.987 --> 00:06:06.421
I started to look at the world
in such a different way,

00:06:06.421 --> 00:06:08.677
more positive, more optimistic.

00:06:08.677 --> 00:06:13.397
I could learn stuff from everyday objects
and everyday activities,

00:06:13.397 --> 00:06:14.867
and this really helped me.

00:06:14.867 --> 00:06:16.866
So I encourage you to do the same.

00:06:16.866 --> 00:06:20.807
And who knows? Maybe it could change
your mindset on looking at life.

00:06:20.807 --> 00:06:23.147
And all of a sudden,

00:06:23.147 --> 00:06:28.056
you will start realizing there's so
much you can learn from everyday objects

00:06:28.056 --> 00:06:31.000
because even the potato chip
was made on a whim.

